		{
			"id": "eG_w5zgYqOKpgWYfC",
			"description": "Ionic Field, Skitarii Pattern",
			"local_notes": "Shield 30 SP. Recharge: after 2 seconds without taking damage, regains 10 SP per second. Ranged attacks only, as the Imperium Refractor Field. Uses the regenerating-shield rules in framework.md (\"Personal force fields\"); the GCS Uses counter tracks current Shield Points.\n\nLore: A rugged, field-forged energy screen issued to some Skitarii officers and Tech-Priest combat-adepts, current Codex Adeptus Mechanicus background describing Mechanicus ionic shields as a coarser, more mass-producible cousin of the Inquisition's refractor fields - dependable and cheap to maintain by Mechanicus standards, but carrying less charge than a hand-fitted Inquisitorial relic.\n\nDesign: Deliberately not identical to the Imperium Refractor Field: that entry is an Inquisition-grade hand-fitted relic (40 SP), while this is described as coarser, mass-forge equipment, so its pool is set below the Refractor Field's at 30 SP with the same fast 2-second/10-per-second recharge (Mechanicus engineering favours reliability and quick recovery over raw capacity, matching \"dependable... but carrying less charge\"). It eats a single lasgun hit (avg 24.5) comfortably and is back to full a few seconds after the shooting stops, but a bolt (avg 42 plus its follow-up detonating on the field) breaks it outright, the same practical ceiling the Refractor Field has against Astartes-tier weapons. Ranged-only, as the Refractor Field, for the same fiction-matching reason (it scatters fast projectiles, not a blade or a punch). TL11/LC1: Mechanicus-manufactured, restricted outside the Cult Mechanicus and its Skitarii per framework.md's legality table, one tier below the Inquisition-issue Refractor Field's own LC1 (that entry sits at the edge of general Inquisitorial-agent issue; this one is Mechanicus internal kit only, reflected in cost rather than LC since both already share LC1).",
			"tags": [
				"Force Field",
				"Energy Shield",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "1",
			"base_value": "6000",
			"base_weight": "3 lb",
			"uses": 30,
			"max_uses": 30,
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eEkgaxS9pBWJI8u2I",
			"description": "Conversion Field, Ordo Reductor Pattern",
			"local_notes": "Shield 75 SP. Recharge: after 3 seconds without taking damage, regains 10 SP per second. Blocks ranged and melee attacks. Flash: any single hit that removes 20+ SP converts into a burst of light; everyone within 5 yards facing the wearer (not the wearer) rolls HT or is blinded for 1d seconds, as the Imperium Conversion Field. Uses the regenerating-shield rules in framework.md.\n\nLore: A rarer, Mechanicus-forged conversion field carried by senior magi of the Ordo Reductor and a handful of other archeotech-obsessed Mechanicus factions, background material treating it as functionally the same relic technology the Inquisition's own conversion fields use, adapted and consecrated by Mechanicus ritual rather than independently reinvented.\n\nDesign: Set just below the Imperium Conversion Field's own 80 SP (75 SP here) rather than matching it exactly: the two fields share the same underlying archeotech principle (hence the identical recharge delay and rate, and the same Flash rule, copied unchanged from that entry rather than re-derived), but this project treats a Mechanicus reproduction of a rediscovered STC-adjacent device as very slightly less perfect than the specific hand-fitted relic the Inquisition entry represents, the same small step already used between the Skitarii Ionic Field and the Imperium Refractor Field above. Checked by exact dice distribution: it absorbs a bolt with room to spare and stops a plasma bolt (6dx4(3) burn ex, avg 84) a little under half the time, just under the Imperium Conversion Field's own figures at 80 SP, keeping it a genuine step below that rarer Inquisitorial relic without being a toy. TL12 (archeotech-adjacent, matching the Imperium Conversion Field's own TL) and LC0 (Ordo Reductor-restricted, one tier more closely held than the general-issue Ionic Field).",
			"tags": [
				"Force Field",
				"Energy Shield",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "12",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "28000",
			"base_weight": "4 lb",
			"uses": 75,
			"max_uses": 75,
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "euf2UAlvfxURvj6oR",
			"description": "Omnispex",
			"local_notes": "A helm-mounted or handheld multi-spectrum sensor package reading motion, heat, atmosphere, vox-traffic and machine-spirit telemetry simultaneously; the bonus above applies to actively scanning an area or a target, not passive Perception.\n\nLore: The omnispex is the Mechanicus's own answer to the Imperium's auspex, current Codex Adeptus Mechanicus background depicting it as a genuinely more capable multi-spectrum instrument built and maintained to Mechanicus ritual-precision standards rather than mass-produced Munitorum kit, standard issue to Skitarii Marshals and Tech-Priest field officers coordinating a cohort.\n\nDesign: Built directly on the Imperium Auspex's own design logic (a flat Search skill bonus per Basic Set's \"instruments help\" convention, B345) rather than a new mechanic, one point higher (+4 vs. the Auspex's +3) to reflect its genuinely superior multi-spectrum sensor suite per the lore's own \"more capable\" framing - the same small, reasoned step this project uses whenever two items share a design but one is explicitly built to a higher standard (compare the Plasma Caliver's higher RoF over the plain Plasma Gun). TL11 and LC1 (Mechanicus-grade instrument, restricted per framework.md's advanced-Imperial bracket) sit one tier above the Auspex's TL10/LC2, reflecting genuinely more advanced and more tightly controlled technology.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "1",
			"base_value": "1800",
			"base_weight": "5 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "Search"
					},
					"amount": 4
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eMW-x-joThkeOziNY",
			"description": "Data-tether",
			"local_notes": "A short cable or short-range noospheric link plugged directly into a Tech-Priest's cranial port, letting the wearer exchange binharic cant and machine-spirit telemetry with one other linked device or Tech-Priest at touch range without speaking aloud. Requires the Noosphere trait (a traits-file matter) to use meaningfully; an unaugmented human can still plug one in but gets nothing out of it beyond a physical data cable's worth of bandwidth.\n\nLore: The data-tether is the humblest piece of Mechanicus communication wargear: a short physical or close-range noospheric link, current Codex Adeptus Mechanicus and Horus Heresy-era background depicting Tech-Priests plugging into machine-spirits, servitors and each other constantly and unremarkably, the digital equivalent of a whispered aside.\n\nDesign: Deliberately not modelled as a bonus to any skill: its entire function is Noosphere-based communication, which this project's traits files (per framework.md's Adeptus Mechanicus section: \"the Noosphere and binharic cant are Telecommunication and a Language\") already grant as a character trait - this equipment entry exists only to give that trait a physical, priceable object to plug into, the same separation of concerns the Servo-arm entry in the melee weapons file uses for its own tool-vs-weapon split. TL11/LC2: common enough Mechanicus kit that it isn't tightly restricted, but still specialist equipment outside general Imperial manufacture.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "250",
			"base_weight": "0.5 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e6IgCjMbd296q4m1R",
			"description": "Data-tether, Enhanced",
			"local_notes": "As the standard Data-tether, but extends the noospheric link to short range (tens of yards rather than touch) and supports a small handful of simultaneous connections rather than one.\n\nLore: Issued to Skitarii Alphas, Tech-Priest field officers and cohort command staff who need to coordinate several linked minds and machine-spirits at once rather than a single one-to-one data exchange, background material treating it as standard mid-tier command equipment within a Mechanicus cohort's hierarchy.\n\nDesign: A straightforward capability step up from the plain Data-tether (more simultaneous links, genuine short range instead of touch) rather than a new mechanic, priced and weighted up accordingly; it remains, as the base Data-tether does, a physical object supporting the Noosphere trait rather than itself granting any skill bonus or communication capability - the trait is what actually lets a wearer make use of the extra range and connections this hardware provides. TL11/LC2 as the base tether.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "900",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eET-2drSffcimvCCm",
			"description": "Data-tether, Broad Spectrum",
			"local_notes": "As the Enhanced Data-tether, but extends the noospheric link to vox-caster range and can relay across multiple hops through other broad-spectrum tethers or a cohort's command Skitarii, functioning as a Mechanicus-internal communications backbone as well as a personal link.\n\nLore: A rarer, higher-output tether carried by Magi and Tacticae liaisons who must coordinate an entire Mechanicus deployment's noospheric traffic - Kataphron husks, servitors, Skitarii cohorts and allied Astra Militarum vox-nets alike - background depicting it as effectively a portable node in the Mechanicus's wider data-network rather than a personal accessory.\n\nDesign: The top of the data-tether family's escalating range/capacity progression (touch to short range to vox-caster range with relay capability), priced and weighted for genuinely rarer, more powerful hardware; TL bumped to 12 (archeotech-adjacent long-range noospheric relay technology, per framework.md's TL table) and LC1 rather than the lower tethers' LC2, reflecting that a device capable of tapping into the wider Mechanicus data-network is more tightly controlled than a personal link. As with the rest of the family, it remains a physical support object for the Noosphere trait rather than a skill-bonus item in its own right.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "12",
			"legality_class": "1",
			"base_value": "3500",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eBYDT2aXuShL8D7Dr",
			"description": "Servo-skull, Utility Pattern",
			"local_notes": "A hovering augmented human skull fitted with grav-plates, a cogitator core and a small sensor suite, used as a mobile assistant, light source, camera and record-keeper; the bonus above applies to library, archive or field-research work the skull assists with, at the GM's discretion for what \"assisting\" covers in a given scene.\n\nLore: Servo-skulls are one of the Mechanicus's most visually iconic and widespread pieces of gear, appearing throughout Imperial background and Black Library fiction from Inquisitorial retinues to hive cathedral-libraries: the skull of a deceased and honoured Imperial citizen, reclaimed, augmented and set to a menial task in perpetual, silent service.\n\nDesign: Built as a flying utility drone rather than copied from any GURPS robot catalogue entry: its mechanical contribution is modelled as a flat Research skill bonus (the same \"instruments/ assistants help\" convention the Auspex and Medi-kit entries already use) since its actual combat or survival utility in most scenes is narrative (fetching, recording, illuminating, relaying messages) rather than something a single stat line can capture, and this project prefers an honest, modest bonus over inventing a full drone-companion subsystem this schema has no clean hook for. TL11 (advanced but common Mechanicus-adjacent tech, seen across the wider Imperium) and LC2 (available to Inquisitorial and other approved retinues, not Mechanicus-exclusive, per its ubiquity in the fiction outside the Cult Mechanicus itself).",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "1200",
			"base_weight": "3 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "Research"
					},
					"amount": 2
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e6JfZJ1FR0QjLhvgJ",
			"description": "Servo-skull, Medicae Pattern",
			"local_notes": "As the Utility Pattern servo-skull, but fitted with a compact autodoc suite (counter-septic sprayers, sealant foam, a diagnostic augur) in place of general research tools; the bonuses above apply to First Aid or Diagnosis rolls the skull assists with.\n\nLore: A common sight trailing Mechanicus and Militarum medicae staff in background material and Black Library fiction, hovering close to a wounded soldier or Tech-Priest to apply emergency treatment or relay vital signs where a human medic's hands are full elsewhere on a battlefield.\n\nDesign: Same design logic as the Utility Pattern (a flat skill bonus standing in for narrative assistance) applied to medical rather than research skills, at a slightly higher cost for the more specialised autodoc hardware; the two-skill bonus (First Aid and Diagnosis both) reflects that battlefield medicine genuinely calls on both, unlike the single-skill Utility Pattern. TL/LC as the Utility Pattern.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "1600",
			"base_weight": "3 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "First Aid"
					},
					"amount": 2
				},
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "Diagnosis"
					},
					"amount": 2
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e8-V9RB9G4N25IylY",
			"description": "Servo-skull, Combat Pattern",
			"local_notes": "Grants the Observation bonus above only while actively escorting or overwatching its owner in a scene, representing its threat-scanning routines rather than passive background benefit.\n\nLore: A rarer, weaponised servo-skull fielded by Inquisitorial retinues and some Mechanicus security details, background depicting it as a hovering sentry and bodyguard drone armed with a small stubber or laspistol mechanism, useful chiefly for covering an ally's back or discouraging a lone assailant rather than as a serious combatant in its own right.\n\nDesign: Adds a small autonomous weapon to the Utility Pattern's own chassis and skill-bonus logic, deliberately kept weak (a light 2d+2 pi stub mechanism, the same round as the Imperium Autopistol's standard ball ammunition, at ST 3 reflecting the tiny mounting a skull-sized drone can actually carry) and fired at its own low effective skill or a heavy penalty when remote-operated, since no depiction treats a combat servo-skull as more than a minor harassment or bodyguard threat - its real value is the Observation bonus while it's on overwatch, not its gun. TL11 and LC1 (a weaponised drone is more tightly controlled than the unarmed patterns) reflect its status as genuine, if minor, wargear rather than a research or medical tool.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Mechanicus"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "1",
			"base_value": "2200",
			"base_weight": "4 lb",
			"weapons": [
				{
					"id": "WqewSxIweTdwKWVqa",
					"sv": 1,
					"damage": {
						"type": "pi",
						"base": "2d+2"
					},
					"accuracy": "1",
					"range": "80/180",
					"rate_of_fire": "3",
					"shots": "12(20)",
					"bulk": "-1",
					"recoil": "2",
					"strength": "3",
					"usage": "Autonomous stubber sting",
					"usage_notes": "Fired by the skull's own limited cogitator-driven targeting, not the owner's own skill: use the skull's own effective skill of 10, or its owner's Guns (Pistol) skill at -4 if the owner is actively directing it (the GM's call on which applies in a given scene), rather than any character's full combat skill.\n",
					"defaults": [
						{
							"type": "dx",
							"modifier": -4
						},
						{
